{"context":"Code-context yang hanya menerima symbol/file focus tidak cukup untuk AI agent. Agent sering mulai dari task natural language dan belum tahu source file yang relevan. Full repository AST parsing untuk semua bahasa menciptakan coupling dan maintenance burden yang tidak perlu. Cursor-like retrieval menunjukkan kebutuhan terpisah antara source discovery dan context expansion.","decision":"Define three separate code-understanding capabilities: code_discover(query, scope, limit) finds candidate files/symbols; code_context(focus, depth, level, options) expands a selected focus into bounded symbol/import/reference/test/config context; code_get(file or symbol handle, offset, limit) retrieves exact source body on demand. Indexing uses a language-agnostic file inventory for the repository plus optional language adapters for symbol/import/reference parsing. Initial and incremental indexing are local derived data, not CKO or exchange artifacts. Discovery uses deterministic lexical, metadata, Git, and available structural signals as the baseline. Optional RAG or external code intelligence may rank candidates only, with explicit provenance/confidence; it must not create canonical relationships or replace deterministic results. Ambiguous candidates must be returned explicitly, not silently selected. Unsupported languages retain file inventory and bounded fallback behavior. Repository-wide inventory scan is acceptable; deep parsing is adapter-scoped, incremental, or lazy according to repository size and language support.","consequences":"Agents can move from task → candidate source → bounded context → exact source without requiring prior symbol knowledge. Separating discovery from expansion keeps contracts testable and prevents a monolithic smart-context tool. Language support becomes incremental instead of an all-language requirement. Discovery quality depends on available signals; lexical fallback is broader but less precise. RAG can improve natural-language candidate ranking but adds nondeterminism, cost, and privacy risk, so it remains optional and non-canonical. EKA remains an orchestration and provenance layer, not a replacement for Cursor/Sourcegraph/native code search.","alternativesConsidered":"Require agent to provide a known symbol/file rejected because it shifts discovery burden back to agent and limits task-level understanding. Parse every language deeply rejected due to coupling and maintenance cost. One monolithic code_context query rejected because discovery, expansion, and exact retrieval have different inputs, guarantees, and failure modes. RAG as primary source rejected due to nondeterminism and hallucinated relationships. Full AST dump rejected as token-inefficient and poor agent interface."}